<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="92989eed">&lt;p&gt;
    Establishing an architecture implementation governance model typically includes:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Implementing a system of controls over the creation and monitoring of all architectural components and activities,
        to ensure the effective introduction, implementation, and evolution of architectures within the organization.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Establishing processes that support effective management of the above&amp;nbsp;activities.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Implementing a system to ensure compliance with internal and external standards and regulatory obligations.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Developing practices that ensure accountability to a clearly identified stakeholder community, both inside and
        outside the organization.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="2f533ee2"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="7d9481a4">It is good practice to define processes agnostic of content (governance material). This allows introduction of new
governance material (legal, regulatory, standards-based, or legislative) without unduly impacting processes. This
content-agnostic approach facilitates a flexible governance model.</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="ae461f61"></attribute>
<attribute name="purpose" nodeId="1914eba7">To have the necessary governance controls in place for managing and controlling the development, implementation and
deployment of architectures within an organization.</attribute>
<attribute name="alternatives" nodeId="721cbcd1"></attribute>
